257\n11.4 Frege’s deﬁnition of cardinal numbers\n259\nAppendix 11.1 The principle of induction and Peano’s postulates\n262\n12. Numbers, Sets and Inﬁnity. A Conceptual Breakthrough at\nthe Turn of the Twentieth Century\n265\n12.1 Dedekind, Cantor and the inﬁnite\n266\n12.2 Inﬁnities of various sizes\n269\n12.3 Cantor’s transﬁnite ordinals\n277\n12.4 Troubles in paradise\n280\nAppendix 12.1 Proof that the set of algebraic numbers is countable\n287\n13. Epilogue: Numbers in Historical Perspective\n291\nReferences and Suggestions for Further Reading\n295\nName Index\n303\nSubject Index\n306\nCONTENTS\n|\nxiii--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nToday Stripe Press publishes one of my favorite books to date: Maintenance: Of Everything, Part One by Stewart Brand, about the unglamorous yet civilizationally important work of maintenance and… | Tamara Winter | 47 comments (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/tamara-winter-655808122_today-stripe-press-publishes-one-of-my-favorite-activity-7419443704731467776-TfSk)\nciteturn626search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; \n\n# Tamara Winter’s Post\n\nTamara Winter\n\n7mo\n\nToday Stripe Press publishes one of my favorite books to date: Maintenance: Of Everything, Part One by Stewart Brand, about the unglamorous yet civilizationally important work of maintenance and repair. https://lnkd.in/gxrPnBkg Sometimes the proximate cause of a civilization’s collapse is rapid and obvious. But sometimes civilizations unravel slowly because they fail to maintain themselves. Infrastructure decay, increasingly sclerotic institutions, technical debt and, yes, declining birth rates, are all examples of this phenomenon.  I see Maintenance as a natural progression from two other beloved Stewart projects: the Whole Earth Catalog and How Buildings Learn. In this book too, we get to come along as Stewart follows his curiosity to sometimes surprising (and strange) places. Maintenance starts with the 1968 around-the-world Golden Globe race, then moves on to motorcycles, cars, weapons, and more, with long digressions into subjects like rust and repair manuals. The book is laid out accordingly, and reads, as Leah Libresco said in her delightful review, like a late-night conversation with an obsessive friend. Maintenance was *also* our first proper collaboration with Works In Progress. Their team built a tool, Books-in-Progress, for Stewart to draft Maintenance publicly and to receive comments; many of these became marginalia in the print book. You’ll recognize one of my favorite commenters, Brian Potter, as the most recent Stripe Press author of The Origins of Efficiency, which pairs brilliantly with Maintenance. Stripe Press is at its core, fascinated with matters of progress—economic, technological, and, increasingly, moral. But progress is neither inevitable nor permanent. And the power to *maintain*, as Stewart says, is the power to improve. I hope you love this book as much as we do. The team: Devin Jacoviello, Jennifer Yun, Sam Bowman Travis McCall, Rachel Edwards, Robert Kessner, Robert Dolgonos, Pablo Delcan Kevin Wong, Nick Jones, and our very-missed Rebecca Hiscott and Nick Whitaker, had so much fun with this one.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMickleham & Westhumble Local History Group (https://www.micklehamwesthumblehistory.co.uk/shared/attachments.asp?f=38428a1f-b76a-4d7d-9ae8-68317aca0483.pdf&o=M%26WLHG-Newsletter-2022.pdf)\nciteturn626search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; \nplaque although the actual building \nhe knew as home for so long has been \ndrastically re-developed.\nThe new book, to give its full title, Sir \nJames Jeans scientist, philosopher and \nmusician, through space and time \nin the first half of the 20th century \nis written by his son, Christopher \nJeans and the late A. V. Kozenko, \na famous Russian astronomer. \nIt is ‘profusely illustrated using \ncontemporary photographs, many \nset in the Dorking area, from \nfamily albums and other sources.’\nSir James Jeans, although a shy \nand very private man was the \nProfessor Brian Cox of his day. He \nwas described as ‘mathematician, \nastronomer, physicist, musician, a \nbrilliant writer and lecturer, one \nwho could truly be described as \na Renaissance man’. He helped \nlay the foundations of modern \nastrophysics and fascinated the world \nwith his popular books explaining the \nphysical nature of the universe. He \nstimulated future generations with \nan enthusiasm for ‘The Mysterious \nUniverse’, a system of which the planet \nearth is but a minute speck.\nThis biography describes Sir James' \ncontribution to science – astrophysics \n"
